00:05:57.200 --> 00:05:59.930
And then I'll go into a little
bit more detail on the back end

00:05:59.930 --> 00:06:06.770
and how we built our, like,
fake VM via serverless model.

00:06:06.770 --> 00:06:11.790
So what we want is we want a
sandbox to execute code in.

00:06:11.790 --> 00:06:17.910
We want to execute unsafe code
that was given by our users.

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:20.700
And it needs to have
access to system resources.

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:23.450
Anything that you can do beyond
the simple case of practicing

00:06:23.450 --> 00:06:26.690
a program, you have to
actually use system resources.

00:06:26.690 --> 00:06:29.510
Network, file system, etc.

00:06:29.510 --> 00:06:30.680
It should work everywhere.

00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:32.240
Should be cross-browser.

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:35.120
And it should load fast, and
should be extremely cheap.

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:38.000
Right now, today, Repl.it
has more than a million

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:42.200
monthly active programmers
writing code on our platform.

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:46.040
If we were to give a VM to
everyone, like, say, Google

00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:51.440
Shell or some of these
VMs with an online editor,

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:53.300
it's going to be
very, very costly.

00:06:53.300 --> 00:06:55.190
And most of our users
don't have the money

00:06:55.190 --> 00:06:56.310
to actually pay for that.

00:06:56.310 --> 00:07:00.670
So our first pass,
we're like, OK.

00:07:00.670 --> 00:07:03.850
Since we want this cheap,
what we're going to do

00:07:03.850 --> 00:07:06.160
is we're going to actually
compile all the languages

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:07.230
to JavaScript.

00:07:07.230 --> 00:07:11.230
We're going to use
Wasm.js and we're

00:07:11.230 --> 00:07:13.660
going to emulate this
[INAUDIBLE] layer on the client

00:07:13.660 --> 00:07:15.790
to make it work on your browser.

00:07:15.790 --> 00:07:17.960
We ran into a lot of problems.

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:20.020
It had a huge bundle size.

00:07:20.020 --> 00:07:23.200
We had a 15 megabyte
JavaScript bundle

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:26.770
for like Python and all the
kind of Unix interfaces.

00:07:26.770 --> 00:07:30.100
And you know if you're
someone with a Chromebook

00:07:30.100 --> 00:07:32.290
somewhere with a
really bad connection

00:07:32.290 --> 00:07:34.345
and you're downloading
this much JavaScript,

00:07:34.345 --> 00:07:36.220
you're going to run out
of memory just trying

00:07:36.220 --> 00:07:38.920
to parse the JavaScript.

00:07:38.920 --> 00:07:41.500
And it also didn't have access
to real system resources,

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:44.980
no matter how much
we tried to fake it.

00:07:44.980 --> 00:07:47.490
So now we moved it
to the back end.

00:07:47.490 --> 00:07:50.810
But that was two--

00:07:50.810 --> 00:07:52.640
that was almost three years ago.

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:55.063
And serverless wasn't
really a thing.

00:07:55.063 --> 00:07:56.480
Like, it started
becoming a thing,

00:07:56.480 --> 00:07:58.850
but it wasn't even on our minds.

00:07:58.850 --> 00:08:03.620
So we had to make something
that was cheap enough

00:08:03.620 --> 00:08:05.570
that we could run.

00:08:05.570 --> 00:08:08.390
So it should feel like a VM.

00:08:08.390 --> 00:08:11.240
But a VM is actually
very costly, right?

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:14.300
So what we did, we're like,
all right, we're gonna do it,

00:08:14.300 --> 00:08:16.970
we're going to make it run
on on-demand containers.

00:08:16.970 --> 00:08:18.680
Containers should be ephemeral.

00:08:18.680 --> 00:08:25.550
Any time we take out
a set of computing--

00:08:25.550 --> 00:08:27.470
anytime you're idle
in the IDE we should

00:08:27.470 --> 00:08:31.520
be able to take your
container from you

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:34.010
and only bring it back when
you're actually executing code

00:08:34.010 --> 00:08:36.049
or you're doing
something in the IDE.

00:08:36.049 --> 00:08:43.490
It shouldn't run on preemptable
Google Compute Engine or AWS

00:08:43.490 --> 00:08:44.480
spot instances.

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:46.970
Basically you want to run
it as cheaply as possible.

00:08:46.970 --> 00:08:49.520
Preemptable compute
is 80% cheaper.

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:50.670
So how do we do that?

00:08:50.670 --> 00:08:54.960
And again, it's this
ephemeral model.

00:08:54.960 --> 00:08:57.220
It should have
reproducible state.

00:08:57.220 --> 00:09:00.570
So let's say we're on
GCP preemptable They

00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:03.000
take one of our
instances and your coding

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:05.580
on that in a container
inside that instances.

00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:08.570
We should be able to
reconstruct the state

00:09:08.570 --> 00:09:13.290
and should not basically
feel that there was a change.

00:09:13.290 --> 00:09:18.930
And it should scale up and
down according to demand.

00:09:18.930 --> 00:09:21.320
So here's the architecture
that we came up with.

00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:24.680
So we started building
this before Kubernetes was

00:09:24.680 --> 00:09:26.630
the phenomena that it is today.

00:09:26.630 --> 00:09:29.390
Maybe if we built it today,
we'd start with Kubernetes.

00:09:29.390 --> 00:09:31.400
But when we started,
we're like, OK,

00:09:31.400 --> 00:09:35.780
we're going to have these
virtual machine instances.

00:09:35.780 --> 00:09:37.780
Inside them we're
going to have Docker.

00:09:37.780 --> 00:09:41.870
We wrote our own container
manager orchestration system.

00:09:41.870 --> 00:09:45.470
We're going to have
the files live on GCS.

00:09:45.470 --> 00:09:48.860
We're going to have some kind
of state and redis and etcd.

00:09:48.860 --> 00:09:52.280
And we're going to have
a Docker image registry.

00:09:52.280 --> 00:09:57.050
And then we're going to have a
proxy that actually, whenever

00:09:57.050 --> 00:09:59.090
a request comes in,
finds the container

00:09:59.090 --> 00:10:01.850
and connects you to
the right container.

00:10:01.850 --> 00:10:05.440
So when we start a REPL, which
is what we call a project,

00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:12.120
a REPL is basically a
union of a Docker image,

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:18.080
a GCS state in terms of files,
and some kind of internal state

00:10:18.080 --> 00:10:21.170
about who the user is,
and authorizations,

00:10:21.170 --> 00:10:23.440
and what collaborators
they have,

00:10:23.440 --> 00:10:26.760
and different things like that.

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:32.640
So that's kind of the high level
over overview of our back end.

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:36.630
To dig deep a little
bit onto the components,

00:10:36.630 --> 00:10:38.610
Conman is our container manager.

00:10:38.610 --> 00:10:43.140
You can think of it as something
like Kubernetes or Docker

00:10:43.140 --> 00:10:43.830
Swarm.

00:10:43.830 --> 00:10:47.370
It figures out what needs to
run, where it needs to run,

00:10:47.370 --> 00:10:51.420
and creates the containers
for those things to run.

00:10:51.420 --> 00:10:56.880
Pid1 is a program that we
inject into the container.

00:10:56.880 --> 00:11:01.620
And pid1 is basically the
inits program that we use.

00:11:01.620 --> 00:11:04.890
Pid1 knows how to
talk our protocol.

00:11:04.890 --> 00:11:07.440
And it's responsible for
reproducing the state when

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:08.502
you get a new container.

00:11:08.502 --> 00:11:09.960
So when you get a
new container, we

00:11:09.960 --> 00:11:13.370
inject this program at
runtime that we call pid1.

00:11:13.370 --> 00:11:14.380
It gets run.

00:11:14.380 --> 00:11:17.430
And now it's negotiating
the state with the client

00:11:17.430 --> 00:11:21.510
in order to reproduce it
to the best of its ability.

00:11:21.510 --> 00:11:23.550
And then Container
Registry, we like

00:11:23.550 --> 00:11:26.880
to support a lot of languages,
a lot of environments.

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:29.730
So for every environment
we create a new container.

00:11:29.730 --> 00:11:32.800
And we're constantly
updating these containers.

00:11:32.800 --> 00:11:35.410
Fixing bugs, releasing
security issues.

00:11:35.410 --> 00:11:41.730
So we use Google Container
Registry to be able to do that.

00:11:41.730 --> 00:11:47.460
And storage, that was like a no
brainer to use GCS for storage.

00:11:47.460 --> 00:11:51.300
For the proxy, you saw me how
when I started a web server,

00:11:51.300 --> 00:11:54.840
it just understood that I want
to serve some external traffic.

00:11:54.840 --> 00:11:57.900
And what we do is we
add some state or redis,

00:11:57.900 --> 00:12:01.000
and then our proxy knows
where to send the traffic.

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:06.030
We get a cert from
Let's Encrypt,

00:12:06.030 --> 00:12:08.920
and we give you
HTTPS on the fly.

00:12:08.920 --> 00:12:11.420
And, finally, the state
where I mentioned it,

00:12:11.420 --> 00:12:15.880
it lives on redis
and the other places.

00:12:15.880 --> 00:12:19.530
[MUSIC PLAYING]

